// Interface-only coupling regression (thick layers).
// RKKY is an areal effect, so thickening the layers must NOT increase the
// coupling. Here each layer is 2 cells thick; only the interface cell carries
// the field, so the layer-averaged field is HALF the single-cell value and the
// total energy is independent of layer thickness.
//   |J/(Msat*dz)| = 2e-3/(1e6*0.9e-9) = 2.22222 T  (interface cell)
//   layer average (2 cells: interface + buried) = 1.11111 T
// Antiparallel layers: E = -J A (m1.m2) = -(-2e-3)(-1) A, A=(8*2e-9)^2=2.56e-16
//   E = -2e-3 * 2.56e-16 = -5.12e-19 J  (same as a 1-cell layer).
SetGridSize(8, 8, 4)
SetCellSize(2e-9, 2e-9, 0.9e-9)
Msat = 1.0e6
defregion(100, layer(0).add(layer(1)))
defregion(101, layer(2).add(layer(3)))
ext_RKKY(100, 101, -2e-3)
m.setRegion(100, uniform(1, 0, 0))
m.setRegion(101, uniform(-1, 0, 0))
Run(0)
ExpectV("B_rkky thick (region 100)", B_rkky.Region(100).Average(), vector(1.11111, 0, 0), 1e-3)
ExpectV("B_rkky thick (region 101)", B_rkky.Region(101).Average(), vector(-1.11111, 0, 0), 1e-3)
Expect("E_rkky thick (thickness-independent)", E_rkky.Get(), -5.12e-19, 1e-22)
